engine: model:blind-entailer
output: |
  body: |
    The statement, `Nat.infinite_setOfPred_prime_modEq_one`, reads: for every natural number k, if k is not 0, then the set of natural numbers p such that p is prime and p is congruent to 1 modulo k is infinite. It quantifies over all natural numbers k with k not 0, and asserts infinitely many natural numbers p that are prime and congruent to 1 modulo k. [C:statement/Nat.infinite_setOfPred_prime_modEq_one]

    The module note states the aim as: for every positive natural number k, infinitely many primes leave remainder 1 on division by k. The formal statement matches it, with "positive" given as "k is not 0" over the natural numbers. [N:aim/infinitely-many-primes-one-mod-k, C:statement/Nat.infinite_setOfPred_prime_modEq_one]

    On checking: the file's text holds no `sorry` and declares no axiom of its own, but it was read by rules as text, and nothing was built or run, so whether Lean accepts it is not known here. Names were matched by spelling, so a link between two declarations may be missing or misdirected. [P:claim/checking, P:assumption/read-as-text]

    Stage 1 is the reduction. To show the set is infinite for k not 0, it is enough to produce, for each bound n, one such prime p with n < p. The obvious route, multiplying known primes and adding one, gives no control over the class modulo k; the file instead evaluates the k-th cyclotomic polynomial, whose prime factors are forced into the class 1. [C:part/one-beyond-every-bound]

    Stage 2 splits off k = 1, where every number is congruent to 1, so any prime at least n + 1 serves. That prime comes from Euclid's infinitude of primes, applied to n + 1. The split is needed because the cyclotomic bound below is stated for k > 1. [C:part/the-modulus-one, C:step/case-k-one, C:reliance/infinite-primes, N:decision/split-off-k-one]

    Stage 3 builds the candidate. For k > 1 put b = k times n factorial, so b is at least 2. The bound q - 1 < |Φ_k(q)|, used with q = b, gives |Φ_k(b)| > b - 1 ≥ 1. The least-prime-factor facts, applied to that absolute value (which is not 1), give a prime p dividing it. Reduction of cyclotomic polynomials from the integers to the integers modulo p, together with the fact that an integer vanishes modulo p exactly when p divides it (here for Φ_k(b)), makes the image of b a root of Φ_k over the integers modulo p. [C:part/the-candidate-prime, C:step/value-exceeds-one, C:step/least-prime-factor, C:step/root-mod-p, C:reliance/cyclotomic-value-bound, C:reliance/least-factor-prime, C:reliance/cyclotomic-reduction, C:reliance/zero-mod-p, C:constant/b-at-least-two]

    Stage 4 puts p past the bound. A cyclotomic root modulo p is a unit, used for b, so p does not divide b. If p were at most n, then p would divide n factorial (m divides n factorial for 0 < m ≤ n, with m = p), and so divide b; hence n < p. The factor n factorial is what makes the prime large. [C:part/past-the-bound, C:step/p-not-dividing-b, C:step/p-above-n, C:reliance/root-coprime, C:reliance/divides-factorial, P:reading/factorial-pushes-past-n]

    Stage 5 fixes the order. Fermat's little theorem in the form of orders, for the image of b (nonzero modulo p), gives that its order divides p - 1. Since k divides b and p does not, k is nonzero modulo p, so the roots of Φ_k there are the primitive k-th roots of unity and b has order exactly k. Hence k divides p - 1, read as p congruent to 1 modulo k through congruence as divisibility, with c = 1, b = p and 1 ≤ p. The congruence comes from the order, not from a search. [C:part/order-exactly-k, C:step/order-is-k, C:reliance/fermat-order, C:reliance/cyclotomic-root-primitive, C:reliance/congruence-as-divisibility, P:reading/the-factor-k-keeps-k-nonzero, P:reading/order-forces-the-congruence]

    Stages 2 to 5 make up the one spine lemma, `Nat.exists_prime_gt_modEq_one`: for every k and n with k not 0, there is a prime p with n < p and p congruent to 1 modulo k. Without it there is no source of such primes at all; it is the whole construction. [C:lemma/Nat.exists_prime_gt_modEq_one]

    Stage 6 is a change of language. `Nat.frequently_atTop_modEq_one` takes each n in turn, weakens n < p to n ≤ p, and folds this, by arbitrarily large witnesses on the natural numbers, into "frequently at infinity". The statement then uses, on the natural numbers, that frequently at infinity is the same as an infinite set. [C:part/back-to-infinitely-many, C:lemma/Nat.frequently_atTop_modEq_one, C:reliance/frequently-unfolded, C:reliance/frequently-infinite, P:reading/only-a-change-of-language]

    The yield goes beyond the statement. For each k > 1 and each n, every prime factor p of |Φ_k(k · n!)| satisfies n < p and p congruent to 1 modulo k. In particular there is such a prime between the bounds: strictly above n and at most |Φ_k(k · n!)|. As a statement it needs two changes: an arbitrary prime factor in place of the least one, since lines 43-59 use only that p is a prime dividing the value; and the fact that the least prime factor of a number greater than 1 is at most that number, for the upper end. With k = 3 and n = 2, b = 6 and Φ_3(6) = 43, a prime above 2 with 43 - 1 = 3 · 14. [P:yield/what-the-proof-delivers, P:case/three-and-two]

    The choices: the evaluation point b = k · n!, where perhaps k times the least common multiple of 1 to n would give a smaller value and a smaller upper end (medium confidence); the least prime factor, which gives the smallest witness; any prime at least n + 1 for k = 1; and each n in turn for the frequently lemma. The slack is high-confidence: "p is the least prime factor" could be "p is any prime dividing the value". [P:yield/what-the-proof-delivers, C:lemma/Nat.exists_prime_gt_modEq_one, N:decision/evaluate-at-k-times-n-factorial, N:decision/least-prime-factor]

    The method reaches only the class 1 modulo k, and says nothing about other classes coprime to k. Open questions: which Lean version and which version of Mathlib the source is written for are still pending, and acceptance by Lean is not known here. [C:statement/Nat.infinite_setOfPred_prime_modEq_one, R:means/lean, C:dependency/mathlib, P:claim/checking]
